1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,466
traceDB: a fast, byte budgeted, content addressed store for agent traces and research data, on

2
00:00:06,466 --> 00:00:08,015
S3 or a local directory.

3
00:00:08,015 --> 00:00:13,501
This is a short tour of how it works, what we measured, and what is still unmeasured.

4
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:16,528
Agents leave behind a lot of data.

5
00:00:16,528 --> 00:00:21,180
Thousands of small trace files, and now and then a log that is gigabytes long.

6
00:00:21,180 --> 00:00:26,011
Storing one S3 object per file means one request per file, and listings are slow.

7
00:00:26,011 --> 00:00:31,617
And an agent that reads a whole log by mistake wastes both its context window and the network.

8
00:00:32,620 --> 00:00:34,668
The layout has three kinds of object.

9
00:00:34,668 --> 00:00:39,428
Packs are immutable, around thirty two mebibytes, made of one mebibyte blocks that are

10
00:00:39,428 --> 00:00:40,812
compressed independently.

11
00:00:40,812 --> 00:00:46,015
Segments are immutable catalog deltas that say which path lives in which pack at which offset.

12
00:00:46,015 --> 00:00:48,671
And a single small HEAD file lists the segments.

13
00:00:48,671 --> 00:00:53,210
HEAD is the only thing that ever changes, and it is updated with compare and swap.

14
00:00:54,210 --> 00:00:55,549
Writes are crash safe.

15
00:00:55,549 --> 00:01:01,330
Packs are written first, then the segment, then the HEAD swap, so a crash leaves only invisible

16
00:01:01,330 --> 00:01:01,817
orphans.

17
00:01:01,817 --> 00:01:07,233
Concurrent writers never block each other: on real S3, 6 concurrent writers lost nothing.

18
00:01:07,233 --> 00:01:10,946
And every read has a byte budget, eight mebibytes by default.

19
00:01:10,946 --> 00:01:16,423
A read over budget fails with a plan that shows the bytes and requests it would have cost.

20
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:18,523
Using it is simple.

21
00:01:18,523 --> 00:01:23,803
Point it at a bucket prefix or a local directory, put a folder, then plan a read to see its

22
00:01:23,803 --> 00:01:28,445
cost without fetching anything, and get just the first four kilobytes of a file.

23
00:01:29,450 --> 00:01:38,634
On real agent trace and repository data, 27,646 files and 670 became 7 packs, at 30 percent of

24
00:01:38,634 --> 00:01:39,904
the raw size.

25
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:47,395
Here are real S3 pulls from a laptop, where each round trip is about seventy milliseconds.

26
00:01:47,395 --> 00:01:51,942
One small file takes 170 milliseconds, which is one round trip.

27
00:01:51,942 --> 00:01:57,860
Five large files, 16.1 mebibytes of raw data, take 108 milliseconds in 4 requests.

28
00:01:57,860 --> 00:02:01,757
A thousand random small files take 1,399 milliseconds.

29
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:08,933
Random small files were the hard case, so we swept two settings: how large a gap between ranges

30
00:02:08,933 --> 00:02:12,052
may be coalesced, and how many parallel workers.

31
00:02:12,052 --> 00:02:17,121
The earlier setting took 1,407 milliseconds with 6.5 times read amplification.

32
00:02:17,121 --> 00:02:23,099
The new defaults, sixty four workers and a sixty four kilobyte gap, take 774 milliseconds at

33
00:02:23,099 --> 00:02:24,658
1.8 times amplification.

34
00:02:25,660 --> 00:02:27,911
Agent sessions grow by appending.

35
00:02:27,911 --> 00:02:33,983
When new bytes start with the stored bytes, traceDB stores only the tail as a new extent.

36
00:02:33,983 --> 00:02:40,327
On the local backend, appending a hundred kilobytes to a forty mebibyte session adds about 49

37
00:02:40,327 --> 00:02:44,148
kibibytes of pack, instead of re storing the whole file.

38
00:02:44,148 --> 00:02:48,582
That is roughly 415 times less, in about 51 milliseconds per put.

39
00:02:49,580 --> 00:02:52,319
Search is built from the store itself.

40
00:02:52,319 --> 00:02:58,877
The version three index is sharded, uses contentless full text search, and dedupes repeated

41
00:02:58,877 --> 00:02:59,238
text.

42
00:02:59,238 --> 00:03:04,860
On the real store it shrank from 4.0 gigabytes to 0.79, a 5.1 times reduction.

43
00:03:04,860 --> 00:03:09,977
A cold first search takes 10.2 seconds, against about 4 minutes before.

44
00:03:10,980 --> 00:03:12,169
Some honest caveats.

45
00:03:12,169 --> 00:03:17,756
The S3 numbers come from a laptop, so network round trips dominate, and an in region benchmark

46
00:03:17,756 --> 00:03:18,648
is still to do.

47
00:03:18,648 --> 00:03:24,116
Recall of the new search index against the old one is 0.49 at ten, on a store that has since

48
00:03:24,116 --> 00:03:24,948
grown by half.

49
00:03:24,948 --> 00:03:30,476
Files that are edited rather than appended are stored whole, and there is no history API yet.

50
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:32,048
traceDB.

51
00:03:32,048 --> 00:03:36,872
The code, the design notes and every benchmark file are in the repo.

